const e=[{id:"l-negligence",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"Negligence, in four questions",minutes:30,summary:"Duty, breach, causation and remoteness, taken in that order — and the two places where Malaysian law has taken its own line.",plants:[],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapters on negligence: duty, breach, causation and defences"}],source:"Caparo Industries plc v Dickman [1990] 2 AC 605 (HL); Majlis Perbandaran Ampang Jaya v Steven Phoa Cheng Loon [2006] 2 MLJ 389 (FC); Lok Kok Beng v Loh Chiak Eong [2015] 4 MLJ 734 (FC); Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee [1957] 1 WLR 582; Rogers v Whitaker (1992) 175 CLR 479 (High Court of Australia); Foo Fio Na v Dr Soo Fook Mun [2007] 1 MLJ 593 (FC); Zulhasnimar bt Hasan Basri v Dr Kuppu Velumani P [2017] 5 MLJ 438 (FC); Civil Law Act 1956 s 12; Limitation Act 1953.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check every case citation above in a report you can hold. Zulhasnimar confines Foo Fio Na to the duty to advise of risks, and leaves Bolam, as qualified, governing diagnosis and treatment. That boundary is the point most likely to move, so check for later Federal Court authority before relying on it as stated.",plate:"scaffold",plateCaption:"Scaffolding over a five-foot way, with the toe board the method statement required and nobody fitted.",readMinutes:12,glossaryDense:"The opening sentences name negligence, plaintiff and defendant for the first time in the course. A passage whose job is to introduce vocabulary is supposed to be full of it.",sections:[{id:"sec-l-negligence-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A junction, a broken leg, and four questions that have to be taken in order.",anchor:"fig-l-negligence-0",h:"Four questions, in order",body:[{t:"p",text:"Faizal is riding home on his motorbike when a car turns across the junction without looking. His leg is broken and the bike is written off. Who pays for that, and for how much of it?"},{t:"p",text:"A claim of this kind is called **negligence**, which is careless conduct causing foreseeable damage in breach of a duty. The person who brings the claim is the **plaintiff**, and the person sued is the **defendant**."},{t:"p",text:"Almost every negligence problem is answered by four questions taken in order. Answering them out of order is the commonest way to make a mess of the whole thing, so establish each one before you move to the next."},{t:"list",items:["Duty — did the driver owe Faizal a duty to take care?","Breach — did the driver fall below the standard the law asks for?","Causation — did that carelessness actually cause the harm, in fact and in law?","Remoteness — was that kind of harm reasonably foreseeable?"]},{t:"p",text:"Damage is required as well. Negligence gives nobody a claim on its own, so no loss means no claim, however careless the conduct happened to be."},{t:"diagram",kind:"flow",title:"The four questions, in order",caption:"The order is not stylistic. Each step assumes the one before it, and an answer that argues remoteness before establishing breach is arguing about nothing.",alt:"Four questions, in order. Duty: was one owed to this claimant? Breach: did the defendant fall below the standard? Causation: in fact, and in law. Remoteness: was the damage of a foreseeable kind?",steps:[{label:"Duty",note:"owed to this claimant?"},{label:"Breach",note:"below the standard?"},{label:"Causation",note:"in fact, and in law"},{label:"Remoteness",note:"damage of a foreseeable kind?"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-negligence-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Duty: foreseeability, proximity, and whether it is fair, just and reasonable to impose one.",h:"Duty",body:[{t:"p",text:"Where the situation is a familiar one, a duty already exists and a single sentence disposes of it. Two road users, a doctor and a patient, an employer and an employee are all settled cases, and Faizal's accident is one of them."},{t:"p",text:"The longer test is reserved for genuinely new situations, where nobody has yet decided whether a duty is owed at all."},{t:"rule",text:"The three-stage test: reasonable foreseeability of harm, a relationship of proximity between the parties, and whether it is fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty.",source:"Caparo Industries plc v Dickman [1990] 2 AC 605 (HL), adopted in Malaysia in Majlis Perbandaran Ampang Jaya v Steven Phoa Cheng Loon [2006] 2 MLJ 389 (FC)"},{t:"p",text:"**Proximity** here means closeness of relationship rather than closeness in metres. It asks how directly the defendant's conduct bore on this particular plaintiff, and it is the middle step of that test."},{t:"p",text:"Running the three-stage test on an ordinary road accident wastes the time you needed for causation. Save it for the situation nobody has met before."},{t:"p",text:"The three-stage test comes from an English case, *Caparo Industries plc v Dickman* [1990] 2 AC 605 (HL). Ampang Jaya is the Malaysian authority to cite here, and it is worth reading rather than citing at second hand. It also deals with the hard question of when a public authority is liable. Lok Kok Beng later revisited the approach and is the other case to know."},{t:"caution",text:"Pure economic loss and psychiatric injury have their own restrictive rules. **Pure economic loss** is money lost where nobody was injured and no property was damaged. These are separate categories rather than harder versions of an ordinary duty, so name them as such instead of running the three-stage test and hoping."}]},{id:"sec-l-negligence-2",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Breach is measured against the reasonable person — and on advising of risks Malaysia left Bolam behind.",h:"Breach",body:[{t:"p",text:"The standard is objective. The question is what a reasonable person would have done in the defendant's position, and the defendant's own inexperience does not lower it. A professional is measured against the reasonably competent member of that profession."},{t:"p",text:"Whether the standard was met is worked out by weighing four things against each other. How likely was the harm, how serious would it be, what would precautions have cost, and how useful is the activity to everybody else?"},{t:"p",text:"Those four factors are how you argue breach instead of merely asserting it."},{t:"example",text:"A contractor leaves an open trench across the five-foot way outside a kedai runcit overnight, with no lamp and no barrier round it. Injury is likely, a broken hip is serious, a cone and a light would have cost almost nothing, and there was no urgency about the work. Breach is straightforward to argue on those four factors."},{t:"p",text:"Doctors are the first of the two places where Malaysian law parts company with English law. The older English test said a doctor was not negligent if a responsible body of medical opinion would have done the same thing. It is called **Bolam**, after the English High Court decision in *Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee* [1957] 1 WLR 582."},{t:"rule",text:"On a doctor's duty to advise of risks, Malaysia has departed from the Bolam test and adopted the approach in Rogers v Whitaker: the question is what a reasonable patient would want to know, not what a responsible body of medical opinion would disclose.",source:"Foo Fio Na v Dr Soo Fook Mun [2007] 1 MLJ 593 (FC), adopting Rogers v Whitaker (1992) 175 CLR 479 (High Court of Australia)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the test is no longer what other doctors would have mentioned. It is what this patient would have wanted to be told before agreeing. The Australian case that rule takes its wording from is *Rogers v Whitaker* (1992) 175 CLR 479."},{t:"caution",text:"Foo Fio Na is a genuine Malaysian departure, and an English textbook will mislead you here. But it is confined to advice and the disclosure of risk. The Federal Court settled that in *Zulhasnimar bt Hasan Basri v Dr Kuppu Velumani P* [2017] 5 MLJ 438. For diagnosis and treatment the Bolam test still applies, subject to qualification."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A surgeon does not mention a small but serious risk before an operation. Most surgeons would not have mentioned it either. In Malaysia, does that answer the claim?",options:["Yes — a responsible body of medical opinion would have done the same","Yes, provided the operation itself was carried out competently","No — the question is what a reasonable patient would want to know"],answer:2,why:"On advice and the disclosure of risk, Malaysia follows Rogers v Whitaker rather than Bolam. The first answer *is* the Bolam test, and it is what an English textbook hands you, which is precisely what makes it the trap. The third changes the subject. Doing the operation well is a different duty from telling the patient what might go wrong."}]},{id:"sec-l-negligence-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Causation is but-for first, then whether anything later broke the chain.",h:"Causation",body:[{t:"p",text:"Factual causation is the but-for test. Would the damage have happened anyway, but for the breach? If it would have happened regardless, the breach caused nothing at all, however negligent it looks."},{t:"example",text:"A doctor negligently fails to examine a patient who is already dying of a poison for which there is no treatment. The failure is plainly a breach of duty. It caused nothing, because the patient would have died in any event, so the claim fails at causation rather than at breach."},{t:"p",text:"Legal causation asks a different question: did something break the chain between the breach and the harm? Lawyers call such a thing a **novus actus interveniens**, which is a new intervening act that breaks the chain of causation."},{t:"p",text:"The new act may be something the plaintiff did, something a third party did, or a natural event. The question is whether it was so unreasonable or so unforeseeable that it lifts responsibility off the defendant."}]},{id:"sec-l-negligence-4",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"The kind of damage must be foreseeable; the extent need not, and the claimant's own fault reduces rather than defeats.",h:"Remoteness, and cutting damages down",body:[{t:"p",text:"Even a breach that caused the harm does not make the defendant pay for everything that followed. The law draws a line at harm of a kind nobody could have seen coming."},{t:"rule",text:"Damage is too remote unless it is of a kind that was reasonably foreseeable. The precise manner of its occurrence and its extent need not be.",source:"The Wagon Mound; standard common law"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the *kind* of harm has to be foreseeable. Exactly how it came about, and how bad it turned out to be, do not."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Faizal was not wearing a helmet when the car turned across the junction. Before you read on — what does that do to his claim?",options:["Nothing — the driver caused the collision, not the missing helmet","It cuts his damages by a share the court thinks fair","It ends the claim, because he was breaking the law himself"],answer:1,reveal:"It cuts them. The other two answers are honest instincts, and the law sits between them. The driver did cause the collision, so the claim survives. But Faizal made his own head injury worse, and the court will not ask the driver to pay for that part of it. Carelessness by the person suing reduces the award instead of defeating it."},{t:"p",text:"Two doctrines then reduce or defeat what is actually recovered. The first is **contributory negligence**, which is the plaintiff's own carelessness reducing the award. It cuts the damages rather than defeating the claim, and in Malaysia it is statutory."},{t:"rule",text:"Contributory negligence: damages are reduced to such extent as the court thinks just and equitable having regard to the claimant's share in the responsibility.",source:"Civil Law Act 1956, s 12"},{t:"example",text:"Faizal was not wearing a helmet. The car driver still caused the collision, but the head injury was worse than it would have been. The court cuts his damages by a share it thinks just and equitable. It does not throw his claim out."},{t:"p",text:"The second is **volenti non fit injuria**, meaning that no injury is done to a person who consents to run the risk. It is a complete defence and correspondingly rare, because it needs real knowledge of the risk and real agreement to accept it."},{t:"caution",text:"Watch the limitation period, which is the period within which an action must be brought. A tort claim is generally barred six years after the cause of action accrued. In negligence that clock starts when the damage occurs, and not when the careless act was done."}]},{id:"sec-l-negligence-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Read Ampang Jaya and Foo Fio Na in full rather than in summary.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are no flashcards or problems for this module yet. When you come to read, read Ampang Jaya and Foo Fio Na in full rather than in summary. Read Zulhasnimar next to Foo Fio Na, because it sets the limit of what Foo Fio Na decided."},{t:"p",text:"Between them they hold both of the places where Malaysian negligence has gone its own way, and both are commonly examined because an English answer gets them wrong."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qng1",q:"What are the four questions in a negligence claim?",options:["Duty, damage, defence, how much money","Duty, breach, causation, remoteness","Foreseeability, proximity, policy, damage","Act, omission, causation, loss"],answer:1,why:"Take them in order, because each one assumes the one before it has been answered.",source:"Standard classification; the elements of the tort of negligence"},{id:"qng2",q:"On a doctor's duty to warn about risks, what has Malaysia adopted?",options:["The Rogers v Whitaker approach — what a reasonable patient would want to know","The Bolam test","A duty to disclose absolutely everything","No duty to advise of risks at all"],answer:0,why:"Foo Fio Na is a real Malaysian departure. Note that it is about advice, not diagnosis or treatment.",source:"Foo Fio Na v Dr Soo Fook Mun [2007] 1 MLJ 593 (FC); Zulhasnimar bt Hasan Basri v Dr Kuppu Velumani P [2017] 5 MLJ 438 (FC)"},{id:"qng3",q:"What has to be foreseeable for damage not to be too remote?",options:["The kind of damage","The precise way it happened","How bad it turned out","Who exactly would be hurt"],answer:0,why:"The manner and the extent of the harm need not be foreseeable. Only the kind of harm must be.",source:"The Wagon Mound (No 1) [1961] AC 388"},{id:"qng4",q:"What does contributory negligence do to a claim?",options:["The claim fails altogether","The damages are cut by the share the court thinks just","It affects only who pays the costs","It moves the burden of proof"],answer:1,why:"It shares out the responsibility, so it is a reduction rather than a defence.",source:"Civil Law Act 1956 s 12"},{id:"qng5",q:"When does the limitation clock generally start in negligence?",options:["When the damage occurs","When the careless act is done","When the plaintiff finds out about the damage","When a lawyer is instructed"],answer:0,why:"Which can be a good deal later than the careless act itself. The general period is six years.",source:"Limitation Act 1953 s 6; in negligence time runs from the damage"}]},{id:"l-vicarious",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"When someone else pays: vicarious liability",summary:"An employer can be liable for a wrong they did not commit, forbade, and knew nothing about. The doctrine is strict, and the argument is about its limits.",minutes:28,readMinutes:9,reading:[{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 3 — the provision under which this doctrine applies in Malaysia at all, including the words limiting received law to local circumstances"},{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapter on vicarious liability, for the Malaysian treatment of the connection question"}],source:"Vicarious liability is received English tort doctrine applying in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3. No case is cited and none is relied on.",verify:"Read section 3 of the Civil Law Act 1956 in the current published version, and check the currency date on the reprint. Then check how the Malaysian courts currently state the connection test before relying on any formulation here.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-vicarious-0",h:"Liable without fault",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Vicarious liability makes one person answer for another's tort without any wrongdoing of their own.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Almost everything else in tort asks what the defendant did wrong. This does not."},{t:"p",text:"An employer may be liable for an employee's tort even though the employer was careful, gave clear instructions, and forbade the very thing that happened."},{t:"p",text:"That is not a loophole. It is the doctrine working as intended, and the first thing to be clear about is that no fault of the employer is required."},{t:"caution",text:"So an answer arguing that the employer took every precaution has argued the wrong point. Precautions matter to primary liability in negligence, not to this."}]},{id:"sec-l-vicarious-1",h:"Why the law does this",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The employer sets the enterprise in motion, profits from it, and can insure and spread the loss.",body:[{t:"p",text:"It is worth being able to say why, because the reasons decide the hard cases at the edges."},{t:"p",text:"The enterprise creates the risk. The employer directs the activity and takes the profit from it."},{t:"p",text:"And there is a blunt practical reason: an injured claimant recovers nothing from an employee with no money, and the employer can insure and price the risk."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Is vicarious liability best justified by fault, by risk-creation, or by who can pay?",options:["Fault — the employer chose the employee","Risk-creation and loss-spreading, with the ability to pay as a practical reinforcement"],answer:1,reveal:"Choosing the employee is a fault-based idea, and it cannot explain liability for a wrong the employer expressly prohibited. The enterprise account can: the employer created the situation in which the wrong became possible. Which justification you accept changes how widely you would extend the doctrine."}]},{id:"sec-l-vicarious-2",h:"Two questions, in order",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"First, is the relationship one that attracts the doctrine; second, was the wrong closely enough connected to it.",body:[{t:"p",text:"The analysis has two stages and they must be taken in order."},{t:"p",text:"Stage one is the relationship. Employment is the paradigm, and the employee or independent contractor distinction does most of the work here."},{t:"p",text:"Stage two is connection. Not every wrong an employee commits is the employer's problem; the wrong has to be sufficiently connected to what they were employed to do."},{t:"list",items:["Was the act a wrongful way of doing something authorised, or something wholly outside the job?","Did the employment create the opportunity, or merely the occasion?","Was the employee pursuing the employer's business at all?"]},{t:"p",text:"Stage two is where the modern arguments are. Doing an authorised task badly is the easy case; deliberate wrongdoing is not."}]},{id:"sec-l-vicarious-3",h:"The frolic, and the deliberate wrong",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"An employee on a personal detour may take themselves outside the doctrine, but deliberate wrongdoing does not automatically do so.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two edges are worth knowing because problems are built on them."},{t:"p",text:"The first is the personal detour. An employee who abandons the job for their own purposes may put themselves outside it, and the question is one of degree rather than a bright line."},{t:"p",text:"The second is deliberate wrongdoing. It is tempting to say an intentional wrong can never be part of the job. That is too quick: the doctrine can reach a deliberate act where the employment created more than a mere opportunity for it."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"An employee deliberately assaults a customer during a dispute about the employer's business. Is the employer automatically outside the doctrine?",options:["No — deliberateness does not end the question; the connection to the employment still has to be assessed","Yes — a deliberate crime cannot be in the course of employment"],answer:0,why:"Treating intention as automatically decisive would make the doctrine easy to escape and would not match how the connection question is asked. The analysis is about the closeness of the tie to the employment, not about the employee's state of mind."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-tort-vic-1",q:"What must a claimant show at the first stage of a vicarious liability claim?",options:["That the relationship between wrongdoer and defendant is one the doctrine attaches to","That the employer was careless in supervising","That the employee cannot pay","That the employer authorised the wrong"],answer:0,why:"Stage one is about the relationship. Carelessness in supervision is a primary negligence claim against the employer, which is a different action with a different test."},{id:"q-tort-vic-2",q:"Why does the enterprise justification explain the doctrine better than a fault-based one?",options:["Because employers are usually insured","Because liability arises even where the employer expressly prohibited the act","Because employees are rarely careless","Because it is easier to prove"],answer:1,why:"A fault account cannot explain liability for something the employer forbade. The risk-creation account can, because the employer set up the activity in which the wrong became possible."}]},{id:"l-nuisance",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"Living next to someone: nuisance",summary:"Negligence asks whether the defendant took care. Nuisance asks whether what the claimant is putting up with is unreasonable. That is a different question with a different answer.",minutes:28,readMinutes:9,reading:[{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 3, as the basis on which this received doctrine applies here"},{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapters on private and public nuisance, and on the remedies available"}],source:"Private and public nuisance are received English tort doctrine applying in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3. No case is cited.",verify:"Read section 3 of the Civil Law Act 1956 in its current published form. Check how the Malaysian courts state the reasonableness factors and the special damage requirement before relying on the account here.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-nuisance-0",h:"The interest being protected",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Nuisance protects the use and enjoyment of land, which is why the claimant's connection to the land matters.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Nuisance is not a tort about carelessness. It is a tort about land."},{t:"p",text:"What it protects is the use and enjoyment of land, which is why the first question is what interest the claimant has in the land affected."},{t:"p",text:"Someone with no interest in the land may be genuinely inconvenienced and still have no claim in private nuisance."},{t:"caution",text:"This catches people out constantly. The instinct is that anyone harmed should be able to sue, and this tort is not built that way."}]},{id:"sec-l-nuisance-1",h:"Unreasonable, not merely annoying",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"The test is whether the interference is unreasonable, judged by locality, duration, intensity and any malice.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Every neighbour is occasionally irritating. The tort catches interference that goes beyond what one must put up with."},{t:"p",text:"Reasonableness here is assessed by the circumstances, and the factors recur."},{t:"list",items:["Locality — what is normal for this kind of area","Duration and frequency — a one-off event is rarely enough","Intensity — how bad, at what times","Any malice — deliberate interference is harder to justify","Whether the claimant is unusually sensitive"]},{t:"p",text:"Note what is missing from that list. The defendant's care is not on it. A carefully run activity can still be a nuisance."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A factory operates to the highest standards and still makes its residential neighbours' lives difficult. Is that a defence?",options:["Yes — the defendant took all reasonable care","No — the question is the effect on the claimant, not the defendant's diligence"],answer:1,reveal:"This is the sharpest difference between nuisance and negligence, and it is where a candidate who has only learned negligence goes wrong. Care answers a question nuisance does not ask."}]},{id:"sec-l-nuisance-2",h:"Public nuisance is a different thing",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Public nuisance affects a class of the public, and a private claimant must show damage over and above the rest.",body:[{t:"p",text:"The name suggests a bigger version of the same tort. It is not."},{t:"p",text:"Public nuisance concerns interference affecting a class of the public rather than one occupier's land."},{t:"p",text:"An individual suing on it must show they suffered damage beyond that suffered by the public generally. Without that, the complaint is a public matter rather than their action."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A road is blocked, inconveniencing a whole neighbourhood including your client. Can your client sue in public nuisance?",options:["Yes — they were inconvenienced","Only if they can show damage over and above what the public generally suffered"],answer:1,why:"Shared inconvenience is what makes it public. The special damage requirement is what separates an individual's action from a matter for public enforcement."}]},{id:"sec-l-nuisance-3",h:"Remedies, and what a claimant actually wants",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Damages compensate for what has happened; an injunction is usually what stops it, and it is discretionary.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Ask what the client wants. Usually it is not money."},{t:"p",text:"Damages address past interference. They do nothing about tomorrow."},{t:"p",text:"An injunction is the remedy that changes the position, and because it is equitable it is discretionary — conduct, delay and proportionality all bear on it."},{t:"p",text:"So a nuisance advice that establishes liability and stops has answered half the question."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-tort-nuis-1",q:"Why is the defendant's care not a defence to private nuisance?",options:["Because the tort asks whether the interference with the claimant's land is unreasonable, not whether the defendant was careful","Because nuisance is a criminal offence","Because care is presumed","Because only intentional acts are actionable"],answer:0,why:"Nuisance and negligence protect different things and ask different questions. An impeccably run operation can still impose an unreasonable interference on a neighbour."},{id:"q-tort-nuis-2",q:"What must an individual show to sue in public nuisance?",options:["That they suffered damage over and above the public generally","That they own affected land","That the defendant acted maliciously","That an injunction is available"],answer:0,why:"Special damage is what converts a public matter into an individual's cause of action. Sharing the general inconvenience is not enough."}]},{id:"l-occupiers",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"Dangerous premises, and who answers for them",summary:"Someone is hurt on land. The question is not who owns it but who controlled it, and what they owed to the person who came on.",minutes:26,readMinutes:8,reading:[{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 3, as the route by which this received doctrine applies here"},{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapter on occupiers' liability, noting how far the Malaysian position follows the English statutory scheme"}],source:"Occupiers' liability as received doctrine applying under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3. Malaysia has no direct equivalent of the English Occupiers' Liability Acts, and none is asserted here.",verify:"Check whether any Malaysian statute governs this area before treating the position as purely common law, and check how the Malaysian courts state the duty to entrants who are not visitors.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-occupiers-0",h:"Occupation is control, not ownership",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The duty attaches to whoever has sufficient control of the premises, which need not be the owner.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Start with the wrong question and the whole analysis goes wrong. It is not who owns the land."},{t:"p",text:"The duty attaches to the occupier, and occupation is a matter of control over the premises."},{t:"p",text:"A tenant may be the occupier. So may a contractor in possession of a site. There can be more than one occupier at once."},{t:"example",text:"A landlord who has let premises and retained no control may not be the occupier of them, while the tenant running a business there is."}]},{id:"sec-l-occupiers-1",h:"What is owed, and to whom",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"The duty is to take reasonable care that a visitor is reasonably safe for the purpose they were invited for.",body:[{t:"p",text:"The duty is a duty of reasonable care, not a guarantee of safety."},{t:"p",text:"And it is tied to purpose. A visitor is owed safety for the purpose for which they were permitted to be there, not for whatever they choose to do."},{t:"p",text:"Someone who enters without permission is in a different category, and what is owed to them is less. That category still attracts some duty; it is not a licence to injure."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A shop customer wanders into a plainly marked staff-only area and is hurt. Does the shop owe them the visitor duty there?",options:["Not for that area — permission defines where and for what purpose they were a visitor","Yes — they were lawfully in the shop"],answer:0,reveal:"Permission has boundaries in space and in purpose. Exceeding them can change a person's category, which changes what is owed. The marking matters because it is what fixes the boundary."}]},{id:"sec-l-occupiers-2",h:"Warnings, contractors and children",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A warning helps only if it actually enables safety; children and independent contractors are treated separately.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Three qualifications do most of the work in problems."},{t:"p",text:"A warning does not automatically discharge the duty. The question is whether it was enough to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe."},{t:"p",text:"Children are owed more, because what is obvious to an adult may not be to them, and an allurement may draw them in."},{t:"p",text:"Where the danger was created by an independent contractor's work, the occupier's position depends on whether it was reasonable to entrust the work and to check it."}]},{id:"sec-l-occupiers-3",h:"Where this sits in the corpus",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"This topic has been taught inside a property lesson, which is the wrong module for a tort duty.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Until now this material appeared only inside a land ownership lesson."},{t:"p",text:"That is the wrong home. The question is a tort duty owed to an entrant, not an incident of title, and a reader looking for it in tort found nothing."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Your client was hurt on premises. What is the first thing to establish?",options:["Who had sufficient control of the premises at the time","Who owns the land"],answer:0,why:"Ownership and occupation come apart routinely, and suing the owner of premises they had let and did not control is a claim against the wrong defendant."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-tort-occ-1",q:"Who owes the occupier's duty?",options:["The registered owner","The insurer","The person who built the premises","Whoever has sufficient control of the premises"],answer:3,why:"Control is the test, and it can rest with a tenant or a contractor in possession. There may also be more than one occupier at the same time."},{id:"q-tort-occ-2",q:"Does a warning notice discharge the occupier's duty?",options:["Always, if it is visible","Never","Only if it was enough to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe","Only for adults"],answer:2,why:"The duty is to make the visitor reasonably safe. A warning that does not achieve that has not discharged it, which is why the content and placement of the notice matter."}]},{id:"l-strict-liability-tort",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"Dangerous things that escape",summary:"A rule that makes a defendant liable for damage caused by something they brought onto land, without proof of negligence. Its conditions are narrow and its survival is contested.",minutes:26,readMinutes:8,reading:[{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 3, and in particular the words limiting received law to local circumstances — they matter more here than in most tort topics"},{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapter dealing with strict liability and the escape of dangerous things"}],source:"Strict liability for the escape of dangerous things is received English doctrine applying under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3. No case is cited and no English authority is named.",verify:"Read section 3 of the Civil Law Act 1956 as currently published. Check how Malaysian courts have treated the non-natural use requirement, and whether the rule is treated here as independent of nuisance.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-strict-liability-tort-0",h:"Liability without negligence",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The rule imposes liability for damage from an escape without requiring proof that the defendant was careless.",body:[{t:"p",text:"This rule exists because negligence sometimes cannot reach an obvious injustice."},{t:"p",text:"Someone brings something dangerous onto their land. It escapes. It causes damage. Proving carelessness may be impossible, because the claimant has no idea what happened inside the defendant's operation."},{t:"p",text:"The rule answers that by making the defendant liable for the escape itself, on stated conditions."},{t:"caution",text:"Strict does not mean absolute. There are conditions to satisfy and defences available, and most claims fail on the conditions rather than on the defences."}]},{id:"sec-l-strict-liability-tort-1",h:"The conditions, each a place to fail",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Bringing and accumulating, a non-natural use, an escape, and damage of a foreseeable kind.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Take the conditions one at a time, because a claim can die at any of them."},{t:"list",items:["The defendant brought the thing onto the land and accumulated it there","The use of the land was non-natural, in the sense of special rather than ordinary","The thing escaped from the defendant's land","Damage of a foreseeable kind resulted"]},{t:"p",text:"The first condition excludes things naturally present. The second is the one that swallows most claims, and its meaning has narrowed over time."},{t:"p",text:"The third requires an actual escape. Damage confined to the defendant's own land is not within the rule."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Water accumulates naturally on the defendant's land and flows onto the claimant's. Does the rule apply?",options:["Yes — water escaped and caused damage","No — nothing was brought and accumulated, so the first condition fails"],answer:1,reveal:"The rule is about things a defendant chose to bring and keep. Naturally occurring accumulation is outside it, which is why the first condition is worth stating explicitly rather than assuming."}]},{id:"sec-l-strict-liability-tort-2",h:"How much of it is left",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"As negligence and nuisance expanded, the rule's practical scope narrowed, and its independence is argued about.",body:[{t:"p",text:"This is where the topic becomes interesting rather than merely technical."},{t:"p",text:"When the rule was formulated, negligence was undeveloped. It has since grown enormously, and so has nuisance."},{t:"p",text:"So one view treats the rule as a species of nuisance dealing with isolated escapes. Another treats it as a distinct principle that still has work to do."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Why does it matter whether the rule is independent or a branch of nuisance?",options:["Because the requirements differ, including whose interest in land is protected and what must be proved","It does not — the outcome is the same"],answer:0,why:"If it is a branch of nuisance, nuisance's requirements come with it — including who may sue. Classification here decides what a claimant has to establish."}]},{id:"sec-l-strict-liability-tort-3",h:"Arguing it in Malaysia",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Received doctrine applies here subject to local circumstances, which is a live qualification for a rule about land use.",body:[{t:"p",text:"This rule comes into Malaysian law as received doctrine, and the reception provision carries a qualification about local circumstances."},{t:"p",text:"That qualification is not decoration. A rule about what counts as a non-natural use of land is exactly the kind that might be applied differently in a different setting."},{t:"p",text:"So an answer should reach the reception point rather than assume the English position transplants whole."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-tort-strict-1",q:"Which condition most often defeats a claim under the rule?",options:["That the use of land was non-natural","That there was an escape","That damage occurred","That the defendant owned the land"],answer:0,why:"The non-natural use requirement has narrowed over time and is where most claims fail. Ordinary use of land, however capable of causing harm, falls outside the rule."},{id:"q-tort-strict-2",q:"Why does 'strict' not mean 'absolute' here?",options:["Because damages are capped","Because it only applies to water","Because conditions must still be satisfied and defences remain available","Because negligence must still be proved"],answer:2,why:"Strictness removes the need to prove carelessness. It does not remove the conditions of the rule, and those conditions are where the argument usually is."}]},{id:"l-trespass-person",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"Assault, battery, and being shut in",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Three torts protect the body and its freedom, and none of them asks whether you were hurt. Trespass to the person is complete the moment the interference happens.",source:"Trespass to the person is received English tort doctrine applying in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3. No case is cited and none is relied on.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for assault and criminal force on purpose; confirm it in the current Penal Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. This lesson states no section number for the powers of arrest and detention on purpose; confirm it in the current Criminal Procedure Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Read section 3 of the Civil Law Act 1956 in its current published form, and check how the Malaysian courts state the totality requirement in false imprisonment.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 3, as the route by which these torts apply in Malaysia at all, including the words limiting received law to local circumstances"},{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapters on trespass to the person and on the defences of consent and lawful authority"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-trespass-person-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Trespass to the person is complete without proof of damage, which is what separates it from negligence.",h:"Three torts that need no injury",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Ravi is queuing at a bank in Klang. A guard grabs his arm, marches him to a back room, and locks the door. Nobody hits him. Nobody hurts him."},{t:"p",text:"Ravi has a claim anyway. Three separate torts cover what happened to him, and not one of them requires an injury."},{t:"p",text:"Negligence gives nobody a claim without damage. Trespass to the person is the opposite. The wrong is the interference itself."},{t:"rule",text:"Trespass to the person is committed by a direct and intentional interference with another person's body or liberty. It is actionable per se, meaning the claimant need prove no damage. The three forms are assault, battery and false imprisonment.",source:"General principle of the law of tort, received in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3."},{t:"list",items:["**Assault** — making someone reasonably apprehend immediate unlawful contact.","**Battery** — actually applying unlawful force to another person.","**False imprisonment** — completely restraining someone's freedom of movement, without lawful justification."]},{t:"p",text:"Ravi may have all three. The grab is a battery. The march to the room may be an assault. The locked door is a false imprisonment."},{t:"caution",text:"These words also appear in the Penal Code, where they carry their own statutory meanings. Do not import a criminal definition into a civil claim."}]},{id:"sec-l-trespass-person-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Assault is about what the victim expects; battery is about what the defendant does to them.",h:"Apprehension, and contact",body:[{t:"p",text:"These two are taught together and are constantly mixed up. Keep them apart by asking where the wrong happened."},{t:"p",text:"Assault happens in the victim's mind. They must reasonably expect immediate unlawful contact. No touch is needed."},{t:"p",text:"Battery happens on the victim's body. Any unlawful contact will do, and it need not hurt at all."},{t:"example",text:"A man raises a parang at Puan Aishah across a coffee shop table. That is an assault. If he then shoves her shoulder, that is a battery as well."},{t:"p",text:"Hostility in the everyday sense is not required. An unwanted touch can be a battery even when it is gentle."},{t:"p",text:"But ordinary life involves contact. Being jostled at a Ramadan bazaar is not a battery, because that contact is generally accepted."},{t:"predict",prompt:"The guard swings a fist at Ravi from behind, and Ravi never sees it coming. It misses. Has the guard committed an assault?",options:["No — Ravi never apprehended the contact","Yes — the guard meant to hit him","Yes — a swung fist is always an assault"],answer:0,reveal:"Assault protects the victim from the fear of contact. A blow nobody sees coming produces no fear. The guard may have attempted a crime, and he commits a battery if he connects. But there is no assault, because the element this tort protects was never touched."}]},{id:"sec-l-trespass-person-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The restraint must be complete, but the victim need not know about it at the time.",h:"Shut in",body:[{t:"p",text:"False imprisonment does not need a prison. A locked room, a held arm, or a refusal to open a gate can each be enough."},{t:"p",text:"Two things do the work. The restraint must be total, and it must be without lawful justification."},{t:"p",text:"Total means there is no reasonable way out. A person who could simply walk away by another route is not imprisoned."},{t:"caution",text:"A reasonable escape route is not the same as any escape route. A window three floors up is not a way out."},{t:"p",text:"The victim need not know at the time. Ravi would have a claim even if he had slept through the whole thing."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A mall guard tells Cik Farah she may not leave until a manager arrives. She stays. She could have walked out of an open door behind her. Is that false imprisonment?",options:["No — the restraint was not total, because a reasonable way out was open","Yes — she was told she could not leave","Yes — a guard has no authority to detain anyone"],answer:0,why:"Being told to stay is not the same as being prevented from leaving. Totality is the element doing the work. The third answer states a rule that is too wide, because lawful justification for detention does sometimes exist."}]},{id:"sec-l-trespass-person-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Consent and lawful authority answer most claims, and the award often marks the wrong rather than a loss.",h:"Defences, and what a claim is for",body:[{t:"p",text:"Most defendants do not deny the contact. They justify it."},{t:"table",caption:"The four justifications that come up most often",columns:["Defence","What the defendant must show"],rows:[["Consent","The claimant agreed to the contact — a patient to an operation, a player to a tackle."],["Lawful authority","A power to detain or to use force, given by statute or by the common law."],["Self-defence","Force used to protect themselves or another, in proportion to the threat."],["Necessity","Action taken to prevent a greater harm, where there was no real alternative."]],alt:"Four defences in a table. Consent: the claimant agreed to the contact. Lawful authority: a power to detain or use force, given by statute or common law. Self-defence: proportionate force against a threat. Necessity: action to prevent a greater harm."},{t:"p",text:"Powers of arrest and detention come from statute, and the officer must stay inside them. Detention that outlives its authority becomes false imprisonment."},{t:"p",text:"Because these torts are actionable per se, a claimant with no measurable loss still recovers. The award marks the wrong rather than replacing money."},{t:"p",text:"Where an official has abused a power, the manner of the wrong can be taken into account. Ask what your client wants, which is often an admission."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-trespass-property",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"Trespass to land, and to goods",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Two torts protect what you possess rather than what you own. Both are complete on entry or interference, with no need to show that anything was harmed.",source:"Trespass to land, trespass to goods, conversion and detinue are received English doctrine applying in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3. No case is cited and none is relied on.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for injunctions on purpose; confirm it in the current Specific Relief Act 1950 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. This lesson states no section number for title and boundaries on purpose; confirm it in the current National Land Code as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Then check whether detinue survives as a separate cause of action in Malaysia before pleading it.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 3, as the basis on which these received torts apply here at all"},{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapters on trespass to land and on the torts protecting goods"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-trespass-property-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The claimant sues because they possessed the land, not because their name sits on the title.",h:"Possession is what is protected",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Maswati comes home to Rawang and finds an excavator parked on her land. The contractor says he has the wrong lot number."},{t:"p",text:"She rents the house. Her landlord owns it. She can still sue, and the contractor's mistake makes no difference."},{t:"rule",text:"Trespass to land is committed by a direct and unjustified interference with land in the possession of another. It is actionable per se. What must be intentional is the act of entry; an intention to trespass is not required.",source:"General principle of the law of tort, received in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3."},{t:"p",text:"Two points in that rule cause most of the trouble. The first is possession, and the second is the mistake."},{t:"p",text:"Possession, not ownership, is the interest protected. A tenant can sue. An owner who has parted with possession usually cannot."},{t:"p",text:"And an honest mistake is no defence. Believing the land was yours does not stop the entry being a trespass."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"The contractor genuinely believed lot 41 was his employer's site. Does that defeat Maswati's claim?",options:["No — he intended to enter that land, and that is the intention required","Yes — he had no intention to trespass","Yes — the mistake was a reasonable one"],answer:0,why:"The tort asks whether the entry was deliberate, not whether the defendant knew he had no right to be there. A mistake may reduce what the court awards. It does not answer the claim."}]},{id:"sec-l-trespass-property-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Land is a volume, and permission that has run out turns a lawful visitor into a trespasser.",h:"Above, below, and staying on",body:[{t:"p",text:"Land is not only the surface. It reaches upward and downward, within limits the law keeps sensible."},{t:"list",items:["An overhanging signboard or crane jib can trespass into the airspace above.","Digging or piling under a neighbour's lot can trespass below the surface.","Leaving rubbish or a vehicle on the land is a trespass by placing a thing there.","Staying after permission ends is a trespass, even though the entry was lawful."]},{t:"p",text:"Aircraft at ordinary heights are a different matter. The law does not treat everyday overflight as a trespass."},{t:"caution",text:"Boundaries and title in Malaysia are governed by the National Land Code, not by this tort. Check the register before arguing about where a line runs."}]},{id:"sec-l-trespass-property-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Three separate wrongs cover goods, and choosing the wrong one changes what the court can order.",h:"Interfering with someone's goods",body:[{t:"p",text:"Goods are protected too, and by more than one tort. The differences matter, because the remedies differ."},{t:"table",caption:"Three wrongs against goods, and what each one is for",columns:["Wrong","What the defendant did","What the claimant wants"],rows:[["Trespass to goods","Directly interfered with goods in the claimant's possession.","Damages for the interference itself."],["Conversion","Dealt with the goods in a way that denied the claimant's rights.","The value of the goods, as though they had been sold."],["Detinue","Refused to return the goods after a proper demand.","The goods back, or their value."]],alt:"Three wrongs against goods. Trespass to goods is a direct interference, and the claim is for the interference itself. Conversion is dealing with goods in a way that denies the owner's rights, and the claim is for their value. Detinue is a refusal to return goods after a demand, and the claim is for the goods or their value."},{t:"example",text:"A workshop in Cheras keeps Encik Zul's motorcycle after he refuses to pay a disputed bill. Zul demands it back and is refused. That refusal after demand is the classic detinue."},{t:"p",text:"Conversion is the serious one. Selling someone's goods, or destroying them, denies the owner's rights completely."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A courier delivers Zul's parcel to the wrong address, and it is never seen again. Which wrong is that closest to?",options:["Trespass to goods, because the parcel was handled","Conversion, because delivering goods to the wrong person denies the owner's rights","Nothing — the courier was merely careless"],answer:1,reveal:"Handing goods to a person with no right to them is a classic act of conversion. The third answer is the tempting one, because carelessness is obviously present. But conversion does not ask whether the defendant meant harm. It asks whether the dealing was inconsistent with the owner's rights."},{t:"caution",text:"Detinue has been abolished in some jurisdictions and survives in others. Check the Malaysian position before pleading it as a separate cause of action."}]},{id:"sec-l-trespass-property-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Damages, an injunction, an order for possession, and a narrow right to act for yourself.",h:"Getting them off, and getting it back",body:[{t:"p",text:"Ask what the client needs. It is rarely only money."},{t:"list",items:["**Damages** for the interference, even where nothing was harmed.","**An injunction** to stop a continuing trespass, granted at the court's discretion.","**An order for possession**, where someone has occupied land and will not leave.","**Self-help**, which the law allows narrowly and plainly distrusts."]},{t:"p",text:"Self-help means acting without a court. You may remove a trespasser, using no more force than is reasonable."},{t:"caution",text:"Self-help goes wrong easily. Excessive force turns the occupier into the defendant, facing a claim in trespass to the person."},{t:"p",text:"Injunctions in Malaysia are governed by the Specific Relief Act 1950. That is statute, and a general account of equitable remedies does not replace it."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-economic-loss",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"Money lost when nobody was hurt",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Negligence pays readily for broken bodies and broken things. It is far more reluctant to pay for money lost on its own, and the reasons for that reluctance are the whole topic.",source:"Caparo Industries plc v Dickman [1990] 2 AC 605 (HL); Lok Kok Beng v Loh Chiak Eong [2015] 4 MLJ 734 (FC); Donoghue v Stevenson [1932] AC 562. The category of pure economic loss is received doctrine applying in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3.",verify:"This lesson deliberately names no authority for the negligent statement principle beyond Caparo; find the leading case and the Malaysian decisions applying it before relying on the account here. This lesson states no section number for the contractual route on purpose; confirm it in the current Contracts Act 1950 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Check in the report what Lok Kok Beng actually decided, and check for later Federal Court authority on the recovery of pure economic loss.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 3, and the words limiting received law to local circumstances — they matter here, because the English position has itself shifted"},{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapters on the duty of care and on negligent misstatement, for the Malaysian treatment of economic loss"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-economic-loss-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Money lost alongside injury or property damage is recoverable; money lost with neither is its own category.",h:"Two kinds of lost money",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Hafiz buys a shophouse in Ipoh. He relies on a surveyor's report saying the structure is sound. It is not, and the building is worth RM180,000 less than he paid."},{t:"p",text:"Nobody was injured. Nothing of Hafiz's was damaged. He has simply paid too much."},{t:"p",text:"That is **pure economic loss**, which is financial loss suffered without injury to the person or damage to property. The law treats it as its own category."},{t:"p",text:"Compare the loss that follows physical damage. A lorry damages a factory's machine, and the factory loses a week of production."},{t:"p",text:"That lost production is **consequential economic loss**. It rides on physical damage, and negligence pays for it in the ordinary way."},{t:"rule",text:"A defendant who negligently causes physical injury or damage to property is liable for the financial loss consequent upon it. Financial loss suffered without any such injury or damage is treated as a distinct category, in which a duty of care is not readily imposed.",source:"General principle of the law of negligence, received in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3."},{t:"p",text:"So the first move is always to classify the loss. Getting that wrong sends the whole answer down the wrong road."},{t:"caution",text:"The snail case, *Donoghue v Stevenson* [1932] AC 562, was about physical harm from a defective product. It decides nothing about money lost on its own."}]},{id:"sec-l-economic-loss-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Indeterminate liability, and the fact that money losses usually already have a home in contract.",h:"Why the courts hold back",body:[{t:"p",text:"The reluctance is not squeamishness. There are three reasons, and an answer that names them argues rather than asserts."},{t:"list",items:["**Indeterminate liability.** One careless act can cost a very large number of people money, in amounts nobody can predict.","**Contract is the usual home.** Parties who deal with each other can allocate financial risk in their agreement.","**Ripple effects.** Physical damage stops somewhere. Financial loss spreads outward through everyone who dealt with the victim."]},{t:"example",text:"A contractor cuts a power cable in Shah Alam. Forty factories lose a day of output. None of them owns the cable, and none of their property is damaged."},{t:"p",text:"Letting all forty recover would make one careless spade very expensive. That is the floodgates worry, stated concretely."},{t:"p",text:"In Malaysia the Contracts Act 1950 governs the agreement route. Ask first whether your client has a contract, because it is usually the better claim."}]},{id:"sec-l-economic-loss-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A negligent statement can found a claim where the maker took responsibility and the reliance was reasonable.",h:"Statements people rely on",body:[{t:"p",text:"There is one well-established route through. It is the negligent statement."},{t:"p",text:"Hafiz's surveyor damaged nothing. He said something careless, and Hafiz relied on it."},{t:"p",text:"The law allows recovery here where the relationship between the parties is a special one. Four things are usually asked."},{t:"list",items:["Did the maker have, or claim to have, special skill or knowledge?","Did the maker know the statement would be passed to this claimant?","Did the maker know it would be relied on for this kind of decision?","Was the claimant's reliance reasonable in the circumstances?"]},{t:"p",text:"Notice the third question. Purpose does a great deal of work in this area."},{t:"rule",text:"Where accounts are prepared for one statutory purpose, the maker does not thereby assume responsibility to a stranger who relies on them for a different purpose, such as deciding whether to invest.",source:"Caparo Industries plc v Dickman [1990] 2 AC 605 (HL)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: a statement made for one job is not a promise to everybody who later reads it. Ask what the statement was for."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Hafiz's surveyor was engaged by the seller, not by Hafiz. Does that end Hafiz's claim?",options:["Yes — only the person who paid for the report can complain about it","Yes — the claim belongs to the seller alone","Not necessarily — the question is whether the surveyor knew a buyer would rely on it"],answer:2,reveal:"The absence of a contract is where this topic starts, not where it ends. The point of the negligent statement route is that it reaches a person who never engaged the maker. What matters is whether the surveyor knew a buyer would rely, and for what."},{t:"caution",text:"A disclaimer in the report changes the analysis. It bears directly on whether responsibility was assumed at all."}]},{id:"sec-l-economic-loss-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Cite a Malaysian decision, because recovery for pure economic loss here has not simply followed England.",h:"Arguing it in Malaysia",body:[{t:"p",text:"This is not an area to answer out of an English textbook."},{t:"p",text:"*Caparo Industries plc v Dickman* [1990] 2 AC 605 (HL) supplies the three-stage duty test used in novel situations. Malaysian courts have worked with it."},{t:"p",text:"*Lok Kok Beng v Loh Chiak Eong* [2015] 4 MLJ 734 (FC) is the Federal Court decision to read on the duty owed by a professional. Read it in full rather than in summary."},{t:"p",text:"Read the Malaysian cases for what they decided, and note their dates. The position has moved, and an old summary may describe a stage that has been superseded."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A client lost money relying on advice and has no contract with the adviser. What should your answer establish first?",options:["That the adviser was careless","That the loss is pure economic loss, and so which route to a duty is in play","That the adviser was insured"],answer:1,why:"Classifying the loss decides everything that follows. Carelessness matters only once you know a duty could exist, and the second answer is what tells you where to look for one."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-multiple-wrongdoers",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"When more than one person is to blame",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Two careless people can produce one injury that cannot be divided, and the victim should not have to work out who did what. The law makes each of them answerable for the whole, then lets them argue among themselves.",source:"Joint and several liability for indivisible damage is received doctrine applying in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3. Apportionment for the claimant's own fault is under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 12(1). No case is cited and none is relied on.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for contribution between wrongdoers on purpose; confirm it in the current Civil Law Act 1956 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. This lesson states no rule or order number for third party proceedings on purpose; confirm it in the current Rules of Court 2012 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Read section 12(1) of the Civil Law Act 1956 in its current published form before relying on the wording quoted here.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 12(1) for apportionment against the claimant, and the provisions on proceedings against and contribution between wrongdoers"},{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapter on joint and several tortfeasors, and on contribution between them"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-multiple-wrongdoers-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Separate careless acts producing a single indivisible injury make each defendant answerable for all of it.",h:"One damage, two wrongdoers",body:[{t:"p",text:"Cik Norlia is waiting at a junction in Seremban. A lorry runs the light and clips a van. The van mounts the kerb and breaks her hip."},{t:"p",text:"Both drivers were careless. Her hip was not broken twice. There is one injury, and nobody can divide it."},{t:"p",text:"Lawyers call defendants like these **concurrent tortfeasors**. Their wrongs are separate, but the damage they caused together is one thing."},{t:"p",text:"That differs from **joint tortfeasors**, who are responsible for the same wrongful act. An employer held vicariously liable is the standard example."},{t:"rule",text:"Where the wrongs of two or more persons combine to cause one indivisible item of damage, each of them is liable to the claimant for the whole of that damage.",source:"General principle of the law of tort, received in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3."},{t:"p",text:"Read that rule again, because it is counter-intuitive. Each defendant answers for the whole, and not for a share."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not confuse this with vicarious liability. There, one person answers for another's wrong. Here, each answers for their own."}]},{id:"sec-l-multiple-wrongdoers-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"She may sue either driver for the full sum, and she can be paid only once.",h:"What that means for Norlia",body:[{t:"p",text:"The practical consequences are large, and they are the reason the rule exists."},{t:"p",text:"Norlia may sue both drivers. She may also sue only one, and recover the whole of her damages from that one."},{t:"p",text:"She does not have to prove what proportion each driver contributed. That question is the defendants' problem, not hers."},{t:"predict",prompt:"The lorry driver has no insurance and no money. The van driver is fully insured. Who carries the risk of the lorry driver being worthless?",options:["Norlia — she can recover only half from the van driver","It is shared out between all three of them","The van driver — Norlia can recover the whole sum from him"],answer:2,reveal:"This is the point of the rule. An injured person should not go undercompensated because one wrongdoer turned out to have nothing. The van driver pays the whole sum, then tries to recover a share from the lorry driver. If that recovery fails, the loss stays with a wrongdoer rather than the victim."},{t:"p",text:"One limit matters. Norlia is entitled to one satisfaction of her claim, and no more than one."},{t:"p",text:"So she cannot collect the full award twice by suing each driver in turn. A judgment against one does not double her money."},{t:"caution",text:"Settling with one defendant can affect the claim against the other. Read the terms of any release with care before signing it."}]},{id:"sec-l-multiple-wrongdoers-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A defendant who pays more than their share can claim contribution from the others, and that is statutory here.",h:"Sorting it out between defendants",body:[{t:"p",text:"The van driver has now paid for damage the lorry driver helped to cause. He is not left carrying it."},{t:"p",text:"A defendant in that position may claim **contribution**, which is a payment from another wrongdoer towards what has been paid out."},{t:"p",text:"The court fixes the amount at what is just and equitable, having regard to each wrongdoer's responsibility for the damage."},{t:"p",text:"In Malaysia this is statutory rather than a matter of common law. It sits in the Civil Law Act 1956. Look the provision up rather than taking a number from a summary."},{t:"p",text:"Procedure matters too. A defendant who wants contribution normally brings the other wrongdoer in as a third party."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"The van driver has paid Norlia in full. What does he do next?",options:["Claim contribution from the lorry driver, in a proportion the court thinks just and equitable","Nothing — he paid, and that is the end of it","Sue Norlia for the amount above his own share"],answer:0,why:"Contribution is the mechanism that makes several liability fair to defendants. The third answer inverts the whole scheme, because the claimant is entitled to keep what she recovered."}]},{id:"sec-l-multiple-wrongdoers-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Divisible damage is split at the start, and the claimant's own share is a different question again.",h:"When the damage does divide",body:[{t:"p",text:"Not every case involves one indivisible injury. Sometimes the damage really does come apart."},{t:"p",text:"Where each defendant caused a distinct part, each is liable for that part and no more. The claimant must then prove who caused what."},{t:"example",text:"Two factories in Pasir Gudang discharge waste into the same river on different days. If the harm from each discharge can be identified, each pays for its own."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not mix this up with contributory negligence. Apportionment between defendants and apportionment against the claimant are separate exercises."},{t:"rule",text:"Where any person suffers damage as the result partly of his own fault and partly of the fault of any other person, a claim shall not be defeated by reason of the fault of the person suffering the damage, but the damages recoverable shall be reduced to such extent as the court thinks just and equitable having regard to the claimant's share in the responsibility for the damage.",source:"Civil Law Act 1956, s 12(1)"},{t:"p",text:"That section is about Norlia's own carelessness. It is not the provision that shares liability out between the two drivers."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-tort-damage",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"Causation, remoteness, and the injured person's own fault",minutes:30,summary:"Duty and breach take you halfway. Causation, remoteness and apportionment under s 12 of the Civil Law Act 1956 are where negligence claims are actually won and lost.",plants:["ms-tort-01","ms-tort-02","ms-tort-03","ms-tort-04"],prepares:["pa-negligence-01"],reading:[{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapters on causation, remoteness and defences"}],source:"Civil Law Act 1956 s 12; The Wagon Mound (No 1) [1961] AC 388 as applied in Malaysia; standard common law on factual and legal causation.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check s 12 and the death-and-personal-injury provisions of the Civil Law Act 1956 against the current text, and check the current Malaysian authority on the duty of care test before assuming any particular English formulation applies here.",plate:"chain",plateCaption:"The chain of causation, and the link a grossly negligent later act may break. Breaking it defeats the claim; contributing to it only reduces.",readMinutes:9,sections:[{id:"sec-l-tort-damage-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"But for the breach, would the damage have happened? If it would, the claim fails there.",h:"Did the carelessness cause the harm?",body:[{t:"p",text:"Proving that somebody was careless is not enough. You must also show that the carelessness made a difference to what happened."},{t:"p",text:"The plaintiff is the person bringing the claim. The defendant is the person being sued."},{t:"rule",text:"The plaintiff must show that, but for the defendant's breach, the damage would not have occurred.",source:"Standard common law test of factual causation; applied in Malaysia"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: take the carelessness out of the story and run it again. If the harm still happens, the carelessness did not cause it."},{t:"example",text:"A man goes to a hospital casualty department after swallowing poison. The officer on duty sends him away without examining him. He dies that night. If he would have died even with treatment, the negligence caused no loss. The claim fails on causation however plain the breach was."},{t:"p",text:"The test breaks down in two familiar situations. One is multiple sufficient causes, where each alone would have caused the damage. The other is scientific uncertainty about which of several exposures caused the injury."},{t:"p",text:"Both have generated exceptional approaches. Identify them as exceptions. Do not use them as the general rule."}]},{id:"sec-l-tort-damage-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A later act may break the chain — a deliberate third-party one usually does, a foreseeable one usually does not.",h:"When something else intervenes",body:[{t:"p",text:"A careless driver leaves Siti with a broken leg. On the way to hospital a second driver crashes into the ambulance. Who answers for what?"},{t:"p",text:"A later act may break the chain. A deliberate wrongful act by a third party usually does. An act that was foreseeable, or one the defendant's own negligence made likely, usually does not."},{t:"p",text:"The plaintiff's own unreasonable conduct can break the chain too. Lawyers call an intervening act that breaks it a *novus actus interveniens*."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not use *novus actus* and contributory negligence interchangeably. The first defeats the claim. The second reduces it. Choosing the wrong one is a substantive error dressed as a vocabulary slip."}]},{id:"sec-l-tort-damage-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The kind of damage must be foreseeable; the extent need not, which is where the eggshell skull sits.",h:"The kind of harm, not how bad",body:[{t:"p",text:"Suppose the harm was caused, and nothing broke the chain. There is still a limit on how far liability reaches."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Ahmad is knocked off his motorcycle and takes a modest knock to the head. Because of a rare condition nobody knew of, he can never work again. What does the careless driver pay for?",options:["The ordinary head injury only — nobody could foresee the rest","Nothing, because it was the condition and not the crash that ruined him","All of it"],answer:2,reveal:"All of it. Foreseeability is about the *kind* of harm, not about how bad it turns out. A knock to the head is foreseeable from a road accident, and the driver gets no discount for finding a fragile victim. The first answer is the fair-minded one. It is also the one the law sets aside."},{t:"rule",text:"A defendant is liable only for damage of a kind that was reasonably foreseeable. Where the kind of damage is foreseeable, it is no answer that its extent was not.",source:"The Wagon Mound (No 1); applied in the Malaysian courts"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: ask whether harm of that *sort* was foreseeable. If it was, the defendant does not escape because it turned out worse than anyone expected."},{t:"example",text:"A workshop leaves a hot pipe uncovered and a worker is burned. Burns are a foreseeable kind of harm from an uncovered hot pipe. The workshop does not escape because the burn turned out far worse than expected."},{t:"p",text:"The 'eggshell skull' principle sits inside this. A defendant takes the victim as found. Foreseeability governs the type of injury; the plaintiff's particular susceptibility governs how bad it turns out to be, and it does not reduce the claim."},{t:"example",text:"Ahmad is knocked off his motorcycle and takes a modest blow to the head. Because of a rare condition he already had, it leaves him unable to work. The driver pays for all of it."}]},{id:"sec-l-tort-damage-3",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Section 12 of the Civil Law Act 1956 apportions rather than defeats — reason to a proportion, do not assert one.",anchor:"fig-l-tort-damage-0",h:"When the victim was partly to blame",body:[{t:"p",text:"A motorcyclist wearing no helmet is knocked down by a car that ran a red light. The car is plainly at fault. But the head injury would have been much less serious with a helmet."},{t:"p",text:"Once, that was fatal to the whole claim. Contributory negligence used to be a complete defence, and the injured person walked away with nothing. One section changed that."},{t:"rule",text:"Where any person suffers damage as the result partly of his own fault and partly of the fault of any other person, a claim shall not be defeated by reason of the fault of the person suffering the damage, but the damages recoverable shall be reduced to such extent as the court thinks just and equitable having regard to the claimant's share in the responsibility for the damage.",source:"Civil Law Act 1956, s 12(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: your own carelessness no longer destroys your claim. It cuts the award by whatever share of the blame is yours."},{t:"p",text:"So the question is now a proportion. A good answer proposes a figure and gives reasons for it, rather than asserting only that damages 'will be reduced'."},{t:"p",text:"Where somebody has died, the same Act governs the claims that follow. There is a claim for the dependants and a claim for the estate. The heads of damage recoverable are constrained by the Act rather than left at large."},{t:"caution",text:"Damages for death and personal injury in Malaysia are shaped by statute in ways that differ from English practice. Check the current text of the Civil Law Act 1956 before stating what is recoverable and how it is calculated."},{t:"compare",title:"Two doctrines that are constantly confused",left:{h:"Novus actus interveniens",items:["A later act breaks the chain of causation.","The defendant is not liable for what follows it.","**Defeats** the claim.","A deliberate third-party act usually breaks it; a foreseeable one usually does not."]},right:{h:"Contributory negligence",items:["The claimant failed to take reasonable care for their own safety.","Section 12, Civil Law Act 1956.","**Reduces** the damages, by a proportion the court finds just and equitable.","Before that section it was a complete defence — older material must be read with care."]}}]},{id:"sec-l-tort-damage-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Duty, breach, factual causation, legal causation, remoteness, damage — then defences.",h:"The order to work in",body:[{t:"p",text:"Take the elements in order and do not skip forward. Duty, breach, factual causation, legal causation, remoteness, damage. Defences and apportionment come last."},{t:"p",text:"The order is not stylistic. Each step assumes the one before it, and an answer that argues remoteness before establishing breach is arguing about nothing."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qtd1",q:"A doctor negligently sends away a patient who would have died anyway. What defeats the claim?",options:["Factual causation — the but-for test","Remoteness","Absence of duty","Contributory negligence"],answer:0,why:"The breach caused no loss, so the claim fails at causation however clear the breach was.",source:"Standard common law on factual causation; the but-for test"},{id:"qtd2",q:"What is the effect of contributory negligence in Malaysia?",options:["The claim is defeated entirely","Damages are apportioned under s 12 of the Civil Law Act 1956","It goes only to costs","It breaks the chain of causation"],answer:1,why:"Section 12 replaced the old complete defence with an apportionment of the damages.",source:"Civil Law Act 1956 s 12(1)"},{id:"qtd3",q:"Which is the correct statement of the remoteness rule?",options:["Both the kind and the extent must be foreseeable","Neither need be foreseeable once breach is proved","Only economic loss must be foreseeable","The kind of damage must be foreseeable; the extent need not be"],answer:3,why:"That is the Wagon Mound test, with the eggshell skull principle sitting inside it.",source:"The Wagon Mound (No 1) [1961] AC 388"},{id:"qtd4",q:"What is the difference between a novus actus interveniens and contributory negligence?",options:["The first defeats the claim; the second reduces it","They are the same thing","The first applies only to third parties, the second only to defendants","The first is statutory, the second is common law"],answer:0,why:"The first defeats the claim and the second reduces it. Using them interchangeably is a substantive error.",source:"Civil Law Act 1956 s 12; standard common law on intervening acts"},{id:"qtd5",q:"In what order should a negligence answer proceed?",options:["Damage, duty, breach, causation","Duty, breach, causation, remoteness, damage, defences","Breach, duty, defences, causation","Causation, duty, breach, remoteness"],answer:1,why:"Each step assumes the one before it. Arguing remoteness before breach is arguing about nothing.",source:"Standard classification; the elements of the tort of negligence"}]},{id:"l-defamation",moduleId:"m07-tort",title:"Defamation: the three elements, and the defences that matter",minutes:25,summary:"Defamatory meaning, reference to the plaintiff, and publication. The Defamation Act 1957 then governs the defences, and the line between libel and slander still does work.",plants:["ms-def-01","ms-def-02","ms-def-03","ms-def-04"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"norchaya-torts",where:"the chapter on defamation"}],source:"Defamation Act 1957, in particular ss 4, 5, 8 and 9; standard common law elements of the tort.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check the current text of the Defamation Act 1957 — section numbering and content should be confirmed — and find current Malaysian authority on the classification of internet publication and on whether Reynolds privilege has been received here.",plate:"press",plateCaption:"The press at work. Publication to one person other than the plaintiff is enough, which is where the tort begins.",readMinutes:6,sections:[{id:"sec-l-defamation-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A false message in a group of forty neighbours: what exactly must be proved?",h:"Three things to prove",body:[{t:"p",text:"A WhatsApp group of forty neighbours gets a message. 'Careful with Puan Nora — she takes money from the surau fund.' It is not true. What must Nora show before she can sue?"},{t:"list",items:["**The words are defamatory.** They tend to lower her in the estimation of right-thinking members of society generally. Or they expose her to hatred, ridicule or contempt, or cause her to be shunned or avoided.","**The words refer to her.** Naming her is not necessary. It is enough that reasonable readers, knowing the surrounding circumstances, would understand the words as referring to her.","**The words were published.** Published here means communicated to at least one person other than her. Forty neighbours is thirty-nine more than she needs."]},{t:"p",text:"All three must be established before any defence is reached. An answer that opens with qualified privilege has assumed the case it ought to be proving."},{t:"diagram",kind:"flow",title:"The order the questions come in",caption:"All three elements come before any defence. An answer that opens with qualified privilege has assumed the case it was supposed to prove.",alt:"Five stages in order. The words are defamatory. They refer to the plaintiff. They were published to at least one other person. Only then are the defences reached. Malice comes last, and only against qualified privilege and fair comment.",steps:[{label:"Defamatory",note:"tends to lower her in others' estimation"},{label:"Refers to her",note:"naming her is not necessary"},{label:"Published",note:"to at least one person other than her"},{label:"Then the defences",note:"and not before"},{label:"Then malice",note:"only against qualified privilege and fair comment"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-defamation-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Libel is permanent, slander transient — and slander usually needs special damage, subject to statutory exceptions.",h:"Libel and slander",body:[{t:"p",text:"Libel is defamation in permanent form — writing, print, a posted picture. Slander is defamation in transient form, such as something said out loud."},{t:"p",text:"The distinction still matters. Slander is generally actionable only on proof of special damage, meaning some actual measurable loss. Libel needs no such proof."},{t:"p",text:"The Defamation Act 1957 preserves the categories and modifies them. Two kinds of spoken words are singled out."},{t:"rule",text:"Words spoken and published which impute unchastity or adultery to any woman or girl, and words calculated to disparage a person in any office, profession, calling, trade or business held or carried on by him at the time of publication, do not require proof of special damage.",source:"Defamation Act 1957, ss 4 and 5"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: for those two kinds of spoken words, the plaintiff does not have to show she lost anything. The words are actionable on their own."},{t:"example",text:"A supplier tells three shopkeepers that Encik Faizal, a plumber, does shoddy work and overcharges. Those are spoken words calculated to disparage him in his trade. He does not have to prove he lost a particular job."},{t:"caution",text:"Publication online has made the permanent-or-transient line harder to apply than it sounds. Check the current Malaysian authority on how internet publication is classified before assuming it is libel."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Permanent or transient, and what follows",caption:"The line is harder to draw online than it sounds, and the current Malaysian authority on internet publication is worth checking rather than assuming.",alt:"A question — is the form permanent or transient? — branching two ways. Permanent, such as writing or a posted picture: libel, actionable without proof of loss. Transient, such as spoken words: slander. That generally needs special damage. The exception is words imputing unchastity to a woman, or disparaging a person in their office, profession, calling or trade.",question:"Permanent form, or transient?",branches:[{cond:"permanent — writing, print, a picture",label:"Libel",note:"actionable without proof of loss"},{cond:"transient — spoken",label:"Slander",note:"needs special damage, unless within the Act's two categories"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-defamation-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Justification, fair comment, absolute and qualified privilege, apology and offer of amends.",h:"The defences",body:[{t:"p",text:"Words can be defamatory and still not actionable. The Act and the common law give a publisher several answers."},{t:"list",items:["**Justification** — the imputation is true in substance and in fact. Truth is a complete answer. Under s 8 the defence does not fail merely because the truth of every charge is not proved. That is so where the remaining charges do not materially injure the plaintiff's reputation, given the truth of the rest.","**Fair comment** — an honest comment, on a matter of public interest, based on facts truly stated. Section 9 addresses the position where not every allegation of fact is proved true.","**Absolute privilege** — statements in Parliament and in judicial proceedings, which no malice defeats.","**Qualified privilege** — an occasion where the maker has a duty or interest in making the statement, and the recipient a corresponding interest in receiving it. Defeated by malice.","**Apology and payment into court**, under the Act's own provisions, which have procedural conditions attached.","**Offer of amends**, again on the statute's terms."]},{t:"example",text:"A former employer is asked for a reference and says honestly that the worker was often absent. The employer has an interest in answering, and the new employer an interest in knowing. That is a privileged occasion — unless the former employer was acting out of spite."},{t:"caution",text:"Whether, and how far, the *Reynolds* line of responsible-journalism privilege forms part of Malaysian law has been argued in our courts, and should not be assumed either way. If your answer needs it, cite a Malaysian decision."}]},{id:"sec-l-defamation-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Malice is not an element; it defeats qualified privilege and fair comment.",h:"Malice, and where it bites",body:[{t:"predict",prompt:"The neighbour who sent that message about Puan Nora plainly cannot stand her. Does she have to prove he meant her harm?",options:["No — she never has to prove ill will","Yes — the tort is about attacking a reputation on purpose","Only if the words were spoken rather than written"],answer:0,reveal:"She does not. She proves the three things in the first section and stops. Ill will feels like the heart of it. It is usually why people end up in court. But it is not part of what she must show, and where it does its work is set out below."},{t:"p",text:"Malice is not an element of the tort. A plaintiff does not have to prove that the publisher was hostile."},{t:"p",text:"It matters in exactly two places. It defeats qualified privilege, and it defeats fair comment. Establishing that a publisher disliked the plaintiff does not by itself establish liability."},{t:"p",text:"Damages are at large, meaning they are not tied to a proved figure. The court takes account of the gravity of the imputation, the extent of publication, and the defendant's conduct — including any apology, or its absence."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qdf1",q:"Which three things must a defamation plaintiff prove?",options:["Falsity, malice, damage","Publication, malice, special damage","Defamatory meaning, reference to the plaintiff, publication","Defamatory meaning, malice, damage"],answer:2,why:"Defamatory meaning, reference, publication. Only after all three does any defence arise.",source:"Defamation Act 1957; standard common law elements of the tort"},{id:"qdf2",q:"What does 'publication' mean in defamation?",options:["Printing in a newspaper","Communication to at least one person other than the plaintiff","Circulation to the public at large","Communication to the plaintiff"],answer:1,why:"One third party is enough. It does not require a newspaper or mass circulation.",source:"Standard common law element; publication is communication to one person other than the plaintiff"},{id:"qdf3",q:"Where does malice bite in a defamation action?",options:["It is an element of the tort","It defeats qualified privilege and fair comment","It defeats justification","It removes the need to prove publication"],answer:1,why:"Malice is no part of the cause of action. It answers two of the defences and nothing else.",source:"Standard common law; malice defeats qualified privilege and fair comment"},{id:"qdf4",q:"What is the effect of s 8 of the Defamation Act 1957?",options:["Justification does not fail merely because every charge is not proved true","Fair comment needs no factual basis","Absolute privilege extends to the press","Slander is actionable without proof of damage"],answer:0,why:"It saves the defence where the unproved charges do not materially injure reputation, given the truth of the rest.",source:"Defamation Act 1957 s 8"},{id:"qdf5",q:"Why does the line between libel and slander still matter?",options:["Libel carries a criminal penalty","Slander has a longer limitation period","Libel cannot be justified","Slander generally requires proof of special damage"],answer:3,why:"Slander generally needs proof of special damage, subject to the statutory exceptions such as words disparaging a trade.",source:"Defamation Act 1957; slander generally requires proof of special damage"}]}];export{e as default};
